= Collective intelligence =

'''Collective intelligence''' is the capacity of a group or coordinated system to perform cognitive tasks—such as estimating, solving problems, generating ideas, learning, or making decisions—through contributions from multiple participants. Collective performance can arise from simple aggregation of independent judgments, active collaboration among people with complementary expertise, institutional coordination, or interaction among human and artificial agents.

The term does not imply that a group possesses a single consciousness or mind. Collective intelligence is operationally studied through performance on defined tasks and comparison with individual or alternative group baselines.

== Historical foundations ==

The Marquis de Condorcet's eighteenth-century jury theorem provided an early mathematical result showing conditions under which majority voting among independent voters can become more likely to select a correct alternative as group size increases.

Francis Galton's 1907 analysis of crowd estimates at a livestock competition became a classic illustration of how aggregated judgments can sometimes outperform many individual estimates.

Later work on organizations, cybernetics, social choice, crowdsourcing, distributed computation, and team performance developed different forms of collective problem solving.

The modern psychological literature includes Woolley and colleagues' 2010 report of a general '''collective intelligence factor''' across tasks performed by small human groups.<ref name="woolley">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/20929725/ Woolley, A. W. et al. (2010), “Evidence for a collective intelligence factor in the performance of human groups,” ''Science'' 330(6004), 686–688.]</ref>

== Aggregation and interaction ==

Two mechanisms should be distinguished.

'''Aggregation''' combines judgments produced largely independently. Examples include averaging numerical estimates, majority voting, and probabilistic pooling.

'''Interaction''' allows participants to communicate, divide work, critique one another, create new representations, and adapt their behavior.

Aggregation can exploit independent errors without requiring a group conversation. Interactive groups can solve tasks no member could complete alone, but interaction can also introduce conformity, domination, or correlated mistakes.

== Wisdom of crowds ==

A crowd can outperform individuals when estimates contain partly independent errors.

If one person overestimates while another underestimates, averaging can cancel some error.

The effect depends on conditions such as:

* diversity of information;
* partial independence;
* decentralization of observations;
* an appropriate aggregation rule.

The slogan “wisdom of crowds” should not be treated as a universal law.

A crowd whose members copy the same source may contain many people but little independent evidence.

== Social influence ==

Interaction changes the statistical structure of judgments.

Lorenz and colleagues experimentally showed that even mild exposure to others' estimates could reduce diversity without improving accuracy, thereby weakening a wisdom-of-crowds effect.<ref name="lorenz">[https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/21576485/ Lorenz, J. et al. (2011), “How social influence can undermine the wisdom of crowd effect,” ''Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences'' 108(22), 9020–9025.]</ref>

Social influence can also help. Participants can correct misunderstandings, share unique evidence, and coordinate expertise.

The important question is therefore which communication structure improves performance for the task.

== Collective-intelligence factor in human groups ==

Woolley et al. reported that performance across several group tasks was correlated and could be summarized by a general factor analogous statistically to an individual general-intelligence factor.<ref name="woolley" />

The original study involved 699 participants in small groups. The reported factor was associated with group interaction characteristics including social sensitivity and more equal conversational turn-taking.

Riedl and colleagues later analyzed data from 22 studies involving 5,279 individuals in 1,356 groups and reported evidence that a collective-intelligence factor predicted performance across tasks and out-of-sample criteria.<ref name="riedl">[https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8166150/ Riedl, C. et al. (2021), “Quantifying collective intelligence in human groups,” ''Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences'' 118(21), e2005737118.]</ref>

These findings support a measurable group-performance construct. They do not establish that every group has one fixed intelligence level independent of membership, task, incentives, or context.

== Composition and process ==

Group performance depends on both who participates and how participants interact.

Relevant properties can include:

* individual expertise;
* diversity of information;
* social sensitivity;
* communication equality;
* task allocation;
* leadership;
* motivation;
* shared goals;
* conflict management;
* time constraints.

Riedl et al. reported that collaboration process was an especially important predictor in their combined datasets.<ref name="riedl" />

Composition and process interact. Diversity is useful only if different information reaches the decision process.

== Diversity and independence ==

Diversity can improve problem solving by supplying different perspectives, methods, or information.

But diversity of identity, expertise, data sources, and error processes are different variables.

A group can look heterogeneous while relying on the same evidence source. Conversely, experts from similar backgrounds can possess genuinely independent measurements.

For epistemic performance, independence of evidence should be measured rather than assumed.

== Task decomposition ==

Collectives are especially useful when a task can be decomposed.

A complex project may require:

* domain expertise;
* data collection;
* analysis;
* engineering;
* review;
* communication.

Participants can specialize and integrate partial results.

Coordination cost grows with dependencies among subtasks. Dividing work is useful only if outputs can be combined coherently.

== Crowdsourcing ==

Digital platforms allow large numbers of people to contribute to tasks such as:

* labeling data;
* solving scientific problems;
* reporting observations;
* writing software;
* producing encyclopedic content;
* generating designs.

Crowdsourcing is a method of obtaining distributed contributions. It produces collective intelligence only when the process yields useful group-level performance.

Participation volume alone is not evidence of intelligence.

== Prediction and forecasting ==

Forecasting systems can aggregate beliefs using averages, weighted ensembles, scoring rules, or prediction markets.

Good aggregation requires calibrated participants and mechanisms that preserve useful disagreement.

Forecast quality should be evaluated on held-out outcomes using metrics appropriate to the prediction type.

== Collective intelligence and collective action ==

[[Collective action]] concerns coordination toward shared or interacting goals.

A group can coordinate effectively without demonstrating high cognitive performance, and a group can produce accurate judgments without coordinating broader action.

The concepts overlap when collective decisions guide behavior.

== Collective intelligence and group cognition ==

[[Group cognition]] focuses on cognitive processes occurring in interaction among group members.

Collective intelligence is more directly a performance concept.

A group may exhibit complex group cognition yet perform poorly, or a noninteractive crowd may produce an accurate aggregate estimate without a shared cognitive process.

== Collective intelligence and distributed cognition ==

[[Distributed cognition]] analyzes cognition across people, artifacts, and environments.

Collective intelligence concerns whether the resulting collective performs cognitive tasks effectively.

A cockpit crew or scientific laboratory can be studied through both frameworks, but the questions differ.

== Swarm intelligence ==

[[Swarm intelligence]] is a specific class of decentralized collective behavior inspired especially by social insects and other biological collectives.

It generally relies on local interaction and simple agents producing system-level behavior.

Swarm intelligence is therefore one possible mechanism of collective intelligence, not a synonym for all collective intelligence.

== Multi-agent systems ==

A [[Multi-agent system]] can coordinate autonomous computational agents.

Collective performance can emerge through:

* communication;
* voting;
* auctions;
* shared memory;
* task allocation;
* ensemble inference.

A multi-agent system should be called collectively intelligent only relative to measured task performance.

Merely containing several agents is insufficient.

== Human–AI collectives ==

Human participants increasingly work with search systems, recommender systems, language models, and autonomous agents.

Potential advantages include:

* faster retrieval;
* broader search;
* simulation;
* formal verification;
* parallel work.

Risks include correlated model errors, automation bias, provenance loss, and concentration on shared training data.

A human–AI collective should be compared with human-only, AI-only, and noninteractive aggregation baselines.

== Failure modes ==

Collectives can fail through:

* herding;
* groupthink;
* correlated misinformation;
* domination by high-status participants;
* hidden expertise;
* poor task decomposition;
* incentive conflicts;
* communication overload;
* polarization;
* diffusion of responsibility.

A larger group is not necessarily a smarter group.

== Measurement ==

Collective intelligence should be measured against explicit tasks.

Possible outcomes include:

* accuracy;
* solution quality;
* innovation;
* calibration;
* speed;
* robustness;
* resource cost;
* generalization across tasks.

A claim of collective intelligence is strongest when group performance exceeds appropriate baselines under controlled resource conditions.

== Epistemic boundary ==

Collective performance does not establish group consciousness, sentience, or a unitary collective mind.

Terms such as “global brain” or “superorganism” may be useful metaphors in some contexts, but they require separate theoretical and empirical justification.

